// A NEGATIVE INTERMEDIATE IN A NON-NEGATIVE RANGE ABOVE THE WORD IS ℤ's, AND
// THE ANSWER IS THE INTEGER, EVERYWHERE (ADR 0152, amending ADR 0029).
//
// `(int 0 (pow 2 200))` denotes [0, 2^201). `d` is `a - b`, negative once b
// passes a, and the answer `d + b` is `a` again. Under ADR 0029 every operation
// of the rung was checked against the program's one set, so `d` stopped the
// program at n = 6. Under ADR 0150 a declared set is a claim on the value it is
// declared for, here `calc`'s result, and an intermediate no set claims is ℤ's:
// `d` is −10³⁰, a theorem of its fact, and the answer, a, is in the set. ADR 0152
// checks the set where it claims, so all three answer; with every operation
// fitted again (PlantFitEveryOp) the third stops, which is how the case was.
//
// Before ADR 0029 the fixed limbs trapped (the final borrow of a subtraction) and
// JavaScript trapped (its shift floors a negative to -1n), while Go's `BitLen`
// and Java's `bitLength` read a magnitude and printed a. Whether a program
// answered depended on where it ran and how its integers were stored.
//
//
// On windows, which has no bignum, rendering is ℤ's at each sort (ADR 0095): the
// limb library's render on a value held as limbs, ι of its digits, and the
// word's digit loop (targets/llvm/lang.oro) on one the facts keep in the word;
// the case ran there first in llvm-render-2026-11-07.
//
// big-repr: both
// inputs: 0 5 6
// prints: string
// expect: 5000000000000000000000000000000 5000000000000000000000000000000 5000000000000000000000000000000
export fn calc(n ∈ 0..6) (0..2^200) {
    a = 5000000000000000000000000000000
    b = 1000000000000000000000000000000 * n
    d = a - b
    d + b
}

fn run(n) { decimal(calc(n)) }
